===== Currencies & Progression

+ *Units* — the everyday currency of No Man's Sky. Earned by selling resources, discoveries, and missions; spent on ships, supplies, and upgrades.
+ *Nanites* — the upgrade currency. Earned by uploading scanned discoveries, completing missions, and some NPC interactions; spent on technology and upgrade modules.
+ *Quicksilver* — the Anomaly's special currency. Earned from Nexus missions and expeditions; spent on cosmetic and exotic items.
+ *Ship classes* — C, B, A, and S. Class determines a ship's stat potential; S-class is the rarest and best. The class hunt is a core endgame loop.
+ *Drop pod* — a planetary shelter that grants a free exosuit inventory slot. One of the main ways to expand your carry capacity.

===== Equipment

+ *Exosuit* — your body: inventory, tech slots, life support, hazard protection. Upgrading it is the highest-value investment in the game.
+ *Multi-tool* — your tool and weapon: mining beam, scanner, and weapons in one package. Classes and types vary (pistol, rifle, experimental, alien).
+ *Starship* — your personal ship, used for atmospheric and space flight. Classes and types vary; the launch thruster and pulse engine are its baseline systems.
+ *Freighter* — the large capital ship that acts as a mobile base: storage, fleet management, and warp-capable home.
+ *Frigate* — smaller support ships you buy and send on missions from your freighter. Specializations include combat, trade, exploration, industrial, and support.
+ *Exocraft* — ground vehicles (and more) unlocked through base-building quests. Each type serves a different planet-side role.

===== Survival Systems

+ *Life support* — the suit's oxygen-fed life meter. Drains constantly; refilled with oxygen. Empty life support drains health.
+ *Hazard protection* — shields against a planet's hazards (heat, cold, radiation, toxicity). Refilled with sodium; on extreme worlds it depletes fast.
+ *Sentinels* — the galaxy's automated police. They react to mined resources and misbehavior; their wanted level escalates.
+ *Saving* — exiting your ship, save points, and save beacons save the game. No Man's Sky does not autosave on a timer.

===== Flight & Travel

+ *Launch thrusters* — the takeoff system. Needs Launch Fuel (Di-hydrogen + Metal Plating) to leave a planet.
+ *Pulse engine* — the in-system drive for fast travel between planets and within a star system.
+ *Hyperdrive* — the between-systems drive. Requires Warp Cells (Antimatter + Antimatter Housing) to travel to other star systems.
+ *Star system* — a sun and its planets; each system has an economy type and wealth level, and belongs to a faction.
+ *Galaxy map* — the navigation view of all star systems. With the economy scanner, it shows each system's economy and wealth.
+ *Space station* — the orbital hub of a star system: trade terminal, upgrade vendors, teleporter, and missions.
+ *Teleporter* — instant travel between space stations and your bases' teleporters. The fast-travel network.

===== Story & Hubs

+ *Awakenings* — the tutorial quest line that takes you from the wrecked ship to your first space station.
+ *The Atlas Path* — the philosophical main line about the universe's creators; rewards blueprints and lore.
+ *The Artemis Path* — the long-form story campaign that unlocks after your first few hyperdrive jumps.
+ *The Anomaly* — the multiplayer hub you can summon in space: the Nexus mission board, quicksilver shop, and base-building specialists.
+ *Nexus* — the mission board inside the Anomaly. Source of quicksilver and multiplayer missions.
+ *Settlement* — a community you take charge of: construction, production, and standing decisions.
+ *Expedition* — a limited-time structured campaign with its own save slot, goals, and rewards. (In-game content; not to be confused with frigate missions.)

===== Base Building & Economy

+ *Base computer* — the device that claims a plot of land as your base.
+ *Refiner* — turns raw resources into better ones (carbon → condensed carbon, ferrite → pure ferrite).
+ *Extractor* — harvests mineral or gas deposits continuously for automation.
+ *Trade route* — buying goods in systems where they are cheap and selling where they are scarce.
+ *Economy scanner* — a ship technology that reveals a system's economy type and wealth on the galaxy map.
+ *Portal* — a late-game structure that teleports you to specific planets via glyph addresses.
